    "evaluation_sources": {
      "encyclopedia:en:obsidian": {
        "tool": "search_knowledge_lexical",
        "data": {
          "id": "d3a8e60f-b1ca-55d3-ad24-7daec2f82581",
          "title": "Obsidian",
          "text": "**Common name:** obsidian.\n**Rock type:** volcanic glass.\n**Character archetype:** dramatic introvert.\n**Fragility:** 5/5.\n**Care demand:** 2/5.  \n### Geological Profile  \nObsidian is natural volcanic glass. As the melt cools, crystals fail to develop the coarse crystalline structure found in many other rocks. Its characteristic conchoidal fracture produces smooth, curved broken surfaces. These fractures can create extremely sharp edges. [3, 4]  \n### Physical Properties  \n- **Structure:** predominantly glassy; inclusions, bands, and small crystalline formations may occur.\n- **Surface:** smooth and shiny on a fresh fracture; an untreated piece is not necessarily smooth around its entire perimeter.\n- **Feel in the hand:** dense and cool to the touch at room temperature; thin edges require caution.\n- **Hardness:** approximately 5–5.5 on the Mohs scale, which describes resistance to scratching rather than falling. [3]\n- **Main vulnerability:** brittle breakage and sharp chips; a thin edge is much more sensitive than the solid center.  \n### Typical Colors  \nBlack, smoky brown, and reddish brown. Some varieties have snowflake patterns, bands, or golden, silvery, and rainbow reflections. Not every specimen displays these effects: many simply look like deep black glass. [3]  \n### Sunlight and Moisture Resistance  \n**Sunlight:** ordinary light is suitable for examination and display. For home storage, choosing a spot without strong overheating followed by abrupt cooling is sensible. This is a practical precaution for a brittle, glassy specimen, rather than a claim that sunlight will inevitably crack it.  \n**Moisture:** brief contact with water is usually tolerated well. Remove droplets after cleaning to prevent visible marks. In a bathroom, falling onto tile is a more significant concern than humidity alone.  \n### Ideal Places at Home  \n- **Dark corner:** a protected alcove with soft side lighting. The contrast makes its shine more expressive.\n- **Bedroom:** a stable shelf or display case where the stone is rarely handled.\n- **Study:** a separate soft pad, without contact with other hard specimens.\n- **Windowsill:** possible, although a broad interior shelf is usually more convenient and secure than a position near the window's edge.  \n### Psychological Vibe  \nObsidian sits in the corner wearing black, reflects half the room, and pretends it accidentally became the most striking object in the interior. Its vibe is a nighttime stage, subdued lighting, and a conversation after midnight.  \nIt values personal space: it enjoys being admired, while being passed from hand to hand feels overly familiar. It may look severe, but its main need is simple: set it down carefully and let it shine.  \n### Daily Care  \nNo mandatory cleaning. Fingerprints on a smooth surface can be removed with a soft, clean cloth. Do not run cloth or fingers along an untreated sharp edge.  \n### Monthly Care  \n1. Remove dust with a soft brush or a cloth free of abrasive particles.\n2. If necessary, slightly dampen the cloth and wipe the surface dry afterward.\n3. Inspect the edges for chips without touching sharp areas.\n4. Clean the pad and make sure the stone cannot slide.  \n### Fragility and Drop Behavior  \nA fall onto tile or concrete can produce a major fracture and extremely sharp fragments. Even a small impact may damage a thin projection. After a fall, inspect the stone and the surrounding area before picking it up by a sound, safe section. A rounded, finished specimen is more convenient for frequent tactile interaction.  \n### Fictional Care Ritual  \nOnce a month, give obsidian an evening of side lighting. Let it reflect a lamp while pretending to be an album cover. One compliment is allowed: a second would become a scheduled expectation.",
